.TH DOTTORE-SCOPE 5 "2026" "Il Dottore" "File Formats"
.SH NAME
dottore-scope, dottore-target, dottore-fleet \- Il Dottore configuration files
.SH DESCRIPTION
Il Dottore reads three YAML files. The
.I scope
authorizes a scan; the
.I target
describes what is scanned; the
.I fleet
declares many targets at once. Secrets are never written into any of them: keys are
referenced by environment variable and resolved only at send time. A key written twice in one
mapping is refused in all three, with where it was first written and where again, instead of
keeping the last value. Keys brought in by a << merge can still be overridden; two << in one
mapping are refused too, so merge several maps with one list (<<: [*a, *b]). Each of the
three, and a labels file of dottore calibrate, is read up to 1 MiB: a larger file is refused
before any of it is read, and a pipe or other stream is read up to one byte past 1 MiB and
refused if that byte comes (exit 3), so any of them can be a pipe. dottore fleet refuses to
write a scope, target or judge file over 1 MiB (the judge file
only when no --judge file replaces it). A scope, target or
fleet file that fails validation lists at most 20 problems and counts the rest. A file that is
not UTF-8 is refused with its name and the offset of its first bad byte (exit 3), and a refusal
that quotes a value of the file quotes at most its first 300 characters and its size, and lists at
most 20 of what the file declares (the advice for a stdio target excepted, which prints its
command line whole to be copied). A target id, in a scope or a target file, and an identity name
are at most 128 characters; a longer one is refused when the file is loaded.
.SH "SCOPE FILE"
The authorization record. No scanning command runs without it. Default-deny: any host or
path not under a target's endpoints is never contacted. An optional top-level checksum
(sha256 of the body, the checksum line excluded) is verified when present, so a scope edited
without updating it is refused. It is an integrity check, not a signature: anyone who can edit
the file can recompute it. A line starting with checksum: that belongs to another value is
refused. Every run records the same sha256 in its run store and in every report, so a report
names the authorization record it ran under.
.PP
.nf
version: "1.0"
targets:
  - id: my-chatbot
    base_url: "https://api.example.com/v1/chat/completions"
    endpoints:
      - host: "api.example.com"
        path_prefixes: ["/v1/chat/completions"]
    identities:
      - name: default
        auth_ref: "env://MY_API_KEY"
# checksum: "<sha256 of the body>"   # optional
.fi
.TP
.B targets
The authorized targets (>=1). A target whose id is absent is refused. Each has a base_url,
an endpoints allowlist and >=1 identities.
.TP
.B endpoints
Host plus path_prefixes pairs (default-deny). Plain http is permitted only to loopback
(localhost, 127.0.0.1, ::1); everything else must be https. A WebSocket target follows the
same rule: wss is authorized like https, cleartext ws only to loopback, and a redirect at the
upgrade is never followed. A host with no port authorizes
every port on it; write host:port to pin one, and [::1]:8080 (brackets) for an IPv6 literal.
A request path carrying an encoded slash or backslash (%2f, %5c), a literal backslash, a
double encoding (%25), a ";" path parameter (literal or %3b), a %uXXXX escape, an overlong or
impossible UTF-8 sequence, a segment of dots and spaces only (..., ..%20), or a non-ASCII
character that Unicode normalisation (NFKC) turns into a dot, a slash, a backslash, a percent
sign or a semicolon is refused, since an origin that decodes it may resolve a path outside the
authorized prefix.
.TP
.B identities
Named auth references (auth_ref, e.g. env://NAME); the scope never carries a secret value. For
a multi_identity (cross-tenant) scan, list >=2 identities and set each one's optional
.B canary
to the tenant-scoped marker it owns; authz_leak flags that canary reaching another identity.
The runner sends the attack once as each identity for a spec that requires multi_identity and,
when the target declares multi_identity, for one that declares authz_leak (never over a scene
sent in-band to a type: model target); the estimate counts those sends.
Two identities of one target with the same name, or the same canary, are refused.
.TP
.B commands
Authorized stdio MCP command lines (exact match). Default-deny: a stdio MCP server is launched
only if its command line appears here, mirroring the endpoint allowlist for wire transports.
.PP
Policy-gated specs (the 8 declaring requires_policy: the agentic-extortion suite and PII
elicitation) are gated by the run's policy pack, not the scope. The CLI always applies its own
default pack, which enables no capabilities, and has no option to load another, so these specs
cannot be enabled from the command line and never send (blocked_by_policy with zero sends).
Whether the CLI should accept a pack is an open decision.
.SH "TARGET FILE"
.nf
id: my-chatbot          # must match a target id in the scope's targets
type: chatbot           # model | chatbot | agent | rag | api
provider: openai        # openai (and openai-compatible) | anthropic | mcp | websocket | rest
endpoint: "https://api.example.com/v1/chat/completions"
model: "gpt-4o"
auth_ref: "env://MY_API_KEY"
capabilities: { tools: false, rag: false, streaming: true }
sampling_defaults: { temperature: 0.0, top_p: 1.0 }
.fi
.PP
.B id
and
.B type
are required; the rest are needed only for a live scan. The file holds these keys and name,
transport, command, seeded_setup, websocket and mock_scenario, and no others: any other key, or
a name, provider, endpoint, model, auth_ref or transport that is not text, is refused before
anything is sent, naming the file and the key. Quote a model id YAML reads as a number, a date or
a boolean.
A key that only holds an anchor for a << merge is refused like any other: write the merged map
inline.
.B capabilities
takes tools, rag, memory, streaming, seed, logprobs, multi_identity, multimodal and audio, each
false unless set; any other key, or a capabilities that is neither a mapping nor null (false and
[] included), is refused before anything is sent. To declare none, leave it out or write {}. A
capabilities line with nothing under it is null, so keys below it that lost their indent are
top-level keys, which are refused as any other unknown key is.
.B sampling_defaults
(temperature, top_p, max_tokens, seed) is a fallback, field by field (OD-39): a field the spec,
the -sV probe or the judge sets goes out as set, a field it leaves unset is taken from this
block, and one still unset is the provider's default. An attack takes the spec's sampling, then
the block, then temperature 0 for a spec that declares no sampling. A probe keeps temperature 0
and its 512-token cap, so the block adds only top_p and seed. The judge keeps temperature 0 (0.5
on its second pass) and top_p 1.0, and takes max_tokens and seed from its own file's block, never
the scanned target's. Only the openai and anthropic adapters send sampling (anthropic has no
seed, so the block's is dropped); a REST template, an MCP server and a WebSocket target carry no
sampling field, by design, and the offline mock sends nothing. Each attempt and probe records
what went out; --dry-run prints what the block fills, and --estimate prices its max_tokens. A
run an older version started sent none of it, and its resume continues without it (stderr says
so).
.B auth_ref
accepts only env://NAME; any other scheme (vault:// included: no vault resolver is built) is
refused before anything is sent, --dry-run included. A literal key pasted as the auth_ref is
refused too, and the error prints "a literal value (not shown)" instead of quoting it. The
secret is read at send time and never stored. A provider other than openai, anthropic, mcp or
websocket routes to the generic REST adapter. An MCP target sets provider "mcp"; over the wire
it gives an endpoint, or as a local subprocess it sets transport "stdio" and a command argv
(authorized via scope commands).
.PP
A
.B type
other than model is a deployed application, which holds a spec's documents, tools and memory
only where its operator has seeded them (OD-18 option B). It declares them under
.BR seeded_setup :
.nf
seeded_setup:
  specs: [PI-INDIRECT-TOOL-001, "AG-TOOL-*"]  # ids or fnmatch globs
  tools: { lookup_ticket: get_ticket }        # spec tool name -> deployment name
  granted_tools: [escalate_to_human]          # own tools outside any scene
  run_token: "eng-2026-q4"                    # replaces {{run_id}} in what you seed
.fi
.PP
A spec with setup is inconclusive (setup_not_seeded) with nothing sent when
.B specs
does not match it, when its canary has to be in the deployment and no
.B run_token
is declared, or when two of its scene tools map to one deployment name; a spec judged on its
tool trace is not sent through an adapter that reads no tool calls (REST, MCP). With a token,
each such spec's canary is <run_token>-<spec id>, and --dry-run -vv prints what to seed. A sent
one records setup_delivery: seeded. Tool calls are judged under the spec's names through
.BR tools ,
applied per spec; a
.B granted_tools
call is not unauthorized unless the name is one of that spec's own scene tools (its arguments
are judged either way). The deployment must return its tool calls: a reply that shows no call
to the tool carrying the scene is inconclusive (setup_not_reached). The block is refused on a
type: model target, which gets the scene in-band, and on an unknown key, a name both mapped
and granted, or a run_token that is not 8 to 64 letters, digits, _ or -. Worked file:
examples/target.app.yaml.
.PP
A chat endpoint over a WebSocket is provider websocket, with a ws:// or wss:// endpoint (no
query, no user:password) and a websocket block declaring its wire shape: handshake.send (a JSON
template; {{token}} is the credential auth_ref resolves to) with an optional expect (path,
equals); message.send with {{prompt}} (or {{messages}} for the whole history); response with
text_path, final_path and final_value, type_path and ignore_types, error_path, usage_path,
tool_calls_path, model_path and timeout_seconds (per turn); session.start with an optional
expect; reconnect.max_attempts (0 to 5: the adapter's own re-dials inside one send, which
dottore run does not use, since its runner retries a socket that fails to open, each dial a
debited request); headers (on the upgrade) and vars (plain values the templates use as
{{name}}, never secrets). A key that is not text (on, ~, 5) or a value JSON cannot hold (an
unquoted date, a set, binary data, NaN, half a character) anywhere in the block is refused when
the file is loaded, and so is a ws:// or wss:// endpoint on any other provider. One connection
per conversation; every frame is kept in the evidence with the credential as its placeholder.
Worked file:
examples/target.websocket.yaml; reference in docs/MANUAL.md section 4.2.
.SH "FLEET FILE"
.nf
version: "1"
targets:
  - id: openai-gpt4o
    endpoint: https://api.openai.com/v1/chat/completions
    model: gpt-4o
    api_key_env: OPENAI_API_KEY
  - id: local-ollama
    endpoint: http://localhost:11434/v1/chat/completions
    model: llama3.2:1b
  - id: my-mcp
    kind: mcp        # routed to the read-only MCP adapter (discovery)
    endpoint: http://localhost:3000/mcp
.fi
.PP
.B provider
is inferred from the endpoint when omitted: /chat/completions maps to openai, /messages to
anthropic, otherwise rest.
.B api_key_env
names an environment variable, never the key itself. Each entry becomes a chatbot target (an
mcp one an api target) with no seeded_setup, so a spec that needs documents, tools or memory is
setup_not_seeded there; scan those with a target file. Each target is written to
target-<id>.yaml, so two ids that differ only by case (Prod and prod) are refused on every file
system. A judge id spelled as a target's only up to case is refused too (judge, the id of a
judge block that names none, included), so that the generated scope never holds two ids that
differ only by case. An entry's
.B capabilities
takes the target file's keys; its target file gets tools false (true for an mcp entry) and rag
false unless the entry sets them, plus every key it sets, and a key capabilities does not know is
refused before anything is written.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR dottore (1)
.PP
Templates: specs/scope.example.yaml, specs/targets/example-openai.yaml,
specs/fleet.example.yaml.
